{
  "requirement": "When authoring evidence for a named example, its complete fenced declaration\nMUST be included in the mandatory input set defined by\n{cite}`requirements.authoring.mandatory_inputs`.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Verdict.** The requirement is met. The implementation is correct and marked, and the test covers it, though one assertion is thinner than ideal.\n\n**Implementation.** `AssessmentSources::prepare` in `src/authoring/inputs.rs` carries `{impl}` for `requirements.examples.authoring_inputs`.\n- It accepts any non-term definition as the subject, so named examples are included. It seeds the selector list with `InputSelector::Requirements { requirements: subject_name }`.\n- That selector is resolved by `source_input` and `InputSelector::resolve` in `src/inputs.rs`. For a non-term declaration, `resolve` emits the declaration's `raw_lines`.\n- `collect_definitions` in `src/evidence.rs` builds `raw_lines` from `block.start..block.end`, with only option lines stripped. That range covers the whole fenced block, from the opening `:::{example} name` line to the closing `:::`. The code is not specific to requirements, so it applies to examples as well.\n- The selector is not dropped. `generated_evidence_selector` only filters outline selectors that fall under generated evidence, and it returns `false` for `Requirements` selectors. The selector therefore always lands in `mandatory`. This follows `requirements.authoring.mandatory_inputs`, which `prepare` also implements: deduplication, read denial, and the generated-evidence rules.\n- I found no behaviour in the marked code that the requirement or its sibling requirements fail to specify.\n\n**Tests.** `bundle_combines_direct_references_terms_and_current_existing_selectors` carries `{test}` for this requirement.\n- It declares a `:::{example} case` and calls `prepare` with `requirements.case` as the subject.\n- It asserts that the subject kind is `AssessmentKind::Definition(DefinitionKind::Example)`.\n- It asserts that `mandatory_inputs()[0][\"requirements\"] == \"requirements.case\"`, so the example's declaration is the first mandatory input.\n- It also shows the example appearing as a declaration input in the bundle for `requirements.ready`.\n- The text of the example's input is not asserted directly. Fence-complete text is asserted only for a requirement subject, in `anchored_files_contribute_root_node_inputs` (`:::{req} ready`, body, `:::`) and in `requirements_inputs_omit_option_lines` in `src/evidence.rs`. Both use the shared `raw_lines` path that examples also use. A direct text assertion for an example would be better, but the shared code and the example-subject test together give adequate coverage of a simple requirement.\n\nNo FIXMEs or acknowledged gaps appear in these sources.",
  "references": "b899630e41f440f0f3cdb55b167fbf32959bdbf515b06e0008b680021fa1d4d4",
  "inputs": [
